INT. FERRY GALLEY - ARBITRATION - DAY

A docked ferry's galley, converted for the day. Stainless counters. Sandwich trays. A folding table with three chairs and a pitcher of water.

ARBITRATOR HENRIETTA OSEI-BONSU, 60s, sits at the head. PELAGIA STAVROU, 40s, counsel for the claimant, sits to her left. RHYS MOLYNEUX, 50s, counsel for the respondent, sits to her right. He loosens his tie. She straightens her blazer. Henrietta clicks a pen.

HENRIETTA
Counsel. You've had a week. Is there a deal?

PELAGIA
None, Your Honor.

RHYS
Not at the number she's asking.

HENRIETTA
Which is?

PELAGIA
Two point four million.

RHYS
Preposterous.

PELAGIA ^
It's modest.

RHYS ^
It's insane.

Both stop. Henrietta lifts a hand. Silence.

HENRIETTA
One at a time.

She pours water. Slides the pitcher toward Rhys. He doesn't take it. Pelagia does.

PELAGIA
The respondent's captain falsified the maintenance log. We have the original.

She opens a folder. Slaps a photocopy on the table. He leans in. She leans back. Henrietta puts on reading glasses and studies the page, line by line, as the ferry creaks against its moorings.

RHYS
That's not authenticated.

PELAGIA
It's his handwriting.

RHYS
Anyone can forge handwriting.

PELAGIA
Anyone can try.

Henrietta lifts her head.

HENRIETTA
Mr. Molyneux. Is that your client's signature?

Rhys glances at the page. A long beat. His mouth opens, closes.

RHYS
I would need to confer.

HENRIETTA
Then confer.

He stands. Hesitates. Sits.

RHYS
(quietly)
It's his.

Pelagia exhales. Henrietta takes off her glasses.

HENRIETTA
Break for lunch. Take the sandwiches.

They rise. He holds the door for her. She nods once, without triumph.
